   "final_message": "All 6 sweep points are clean \u2014 DRC 0, LVS match at every point.\n\n## Final Summary\n\nThe `nor2` cell (`Y = !(A | B)`) is built at `/nor2.icl` and verified clean across the full sweep (6/6 points: nominal L=0.15/H=2.72, L=0.18, L=0.25, widths x0.85, widths x1.35/H=3.26, widths x1.9/H=4.08).\n\n**Key design decisions:**\n- **Gate order A, B** for both rows, matching the fixed netlist: p-row `VPWR [A] n1 [B] Y`, n-row `Y [A] VGND [B] Y`.\n- **Diffusion islands**: n row shares VGND in the middle (M1/M2 sources tied together); p row breaks at n1 (M0 drain / M3 source are different nets internally but connected via route).\n- **Internal net n1**: p-row middle drain contact labelled `net=n1`, joined with `route n1 layers=m1,m2` (LVS requires the drain li strips and gate contact li to share the net label).\n- **Output Y**: n-row left/right drains and p-row right drain all labelled `net=Y`, joined with `route Y layers=m1,m2 prefer=m2`; pin goes on the routed met1.\n- **Gate contacts**: bottom derived from n-row items only (`cb = max(nd.top + 0.19, c1l.top + 0.25, ...)`), never p-row, per lesson 1.\n- **width=5 columns** (2.3 \u00b5m) fits at L=0.25 where gates widen.\n\nThe hardest part was correctly labelling the internal net `n1` (p-row middle drain) and output `Y` across both rows so LVS sees a single connected net, and keeping gate-contact bottoms strictly from n-row items to avoid pushing contacts into the p-tap row.",
